"Majlis Pelancaran Laporan Pasaran Harta Separuh Pertama Tahun 2026 (https://mof.gov.my/portal/ms/berita/foto/majlis-pelancaran-laporan-pasaran-harta-separuh-pertama-tahun-2026)\nciteturn207search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 weeks ago; Crawled: today; Sempena Pelancaran Laporan Pasaran Harta Separuh Pertama Tahun 2026 di Institut Penilaian Negara (INSPEN), Selangor, YB Senator Datuk Seri Amir Hamzah Azizan, Menteri Kewangan II menegaskan bahawa asas ekonomi negara yang kukuh serta langkah strategik Kerajaan MADANI terus menyokong kestabilan dan daya tahan pasaran harta tanah. ... Laporan Pasaran Harta H1 2026 boleh diakses melalui portal rasmi NAPIC di napic.jpph.gov.my.\n\n## Foto\n\n# Majlis Pelancaran Laporan Pasaran Harta Separuh Pertama Tahun 2026\n\nFoto 10 September 2026\n\nImage: Majlis Pelancaran Laporan Pasaran Harta Separuh Pertama Tahun 2026\n\nPasaran harta tanah Malaysia terus menunjukkan daya tahan pada separuh pertama 2026, dengan nilai transaksi melebihi RM105 bilion, disokong oleh harga yang kekal stabil, aktiviti pembinaan yang positif serta pertumbuhan dalam beberapa subsektor.\n\nSempena Pelancaran Laporan Pasaran Harta Separuh Pertama Tahun 2026 di Institut Penilaian Negara (INSPEN), Selangor, YB Senator Datuk Seri Amir Hamzah Azizan, Menteri Kewangan II menegaskan bahawa asas ekonomi negara yang kukuh serta langkah strategik Kerajaan MADANI terus menyokong kestabilan dan daya tahan pasaran harta tanah.\n\nAntara perkembangan utama, subsektor kediaman kekal mendominasi pasaran dengan 59.3% daripada keseluruhan transaksi, manakala Indeks Harga Rumah Malaysia mencatat pertumbuhan tahunan 0.9%. Aktiviti pembinaan bagi beberapa segmen termasuk harta tanah kedai, pangsapuri khidmat dan industri turut menunjukkan pertumbuhan positif.\n\nKerajaan terus memperkukuh akses kepada pemilikan rumah, termasuk melalui peningkatan jaminan kerajaan sehingga RM20 bilion di bawah Skim Jaminan Kredit Perumahan (SJKP) serta pelanjutan pengecualian duti setem bagi pembeli rumah pertama untuk hartanah berharga sehingga RM500,000 sehingga 31 Disember 2027.\n\nLaporan Pasaran Harta H1 2026 boleh diakses melalui portal rasmi NAPIC di napic.jpph.gov.my.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMalaysia Launch Index: Launch Conditions by State (H1 2026) » Envicion Studio (https://www.envicionstudio.com.my/malaysia-launch-index/)\nciteturn207search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 weeks ago; Crawled: 3 days ago; Assumption / interpretation (Envicion): the Score weighting (40/30/30); the “launch congestion” reading of Selangor; the RTS and JS-SEZ demand narratives commonly attached to Johor — policy narratives, not NAPIC data. ... Source: NAPIC, Laporan Pasaran Harta H1 2026, released 10 September 2026 (napic.jpph.gov.my).\n\n# Malaysia Launch Index: Launch Conditions by State (H1 2026)\n\nA half-yearly data series by Envicion Studio Research Desk. Every figure below traces to the National Property Information Centre (NAPIC) Property Market Report for the first half of 2026, released 10 September 2026. Methodology is public and stated in full at the bottom of this page.\n\nDownload the full report (PDF)\n\n## The headline: Johor is the easiest state to launch in. Selangor is now the hardest.\n\nDevelopers launched 27,832 residential units in Malaysia in H1 2026 — 25% fewer than a year earlier — and the market still absorbed a smaller share of them: 16.6% sold within the half, down from 22.1%. Completed-but-unsold residential stock rose to 33,094 units (RM17.78 billion), with serviced apartments adding a further 23,375 units (RM19.33 billion). The slowdown was not evenly spread. It split sharply by state — and that split is what the Malaysia Launch Index measures.\n\n## Launch Conditions Score — H1 2026 ranking\n\nThe score runs 0–100; higher means easier launch conditions. It weighs new-launch absorption at 40%, overhang discipline at 30% and competing pipeline at 30%, normalized across the seven states that launched at least 500 units in the half.\n\n#  | State  | Score  | Launch absorption  | Overhang growth YoY*  | Competing pipeline**\n--- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\n1  | Johor  | 86.6  | 36.5%  | +13.1%  | 12,576\n2  | Kuala Lumpur  | 70.6  | 20.7%  | +27.3%  | 4,108\n3  | Pulau Pinang  | 51.8  | 9.8%  | +18.3%  | 9,978\n4  | Perak  | 50.1  | 9.9%  | +25.3%  | 8,926\n5  | Negeri Sembilan  | 48.6  | 12.1%  | +44.5%  | 5,474\n6  | Melaka  | 47.1  | 19.5%  | +73.8%  | 3,374\n7  | Selangor  | 0.7  | 6.8%  | +72.4%  | 24,019\n* Completed unsold units, residential + serviced apartment combined. ** H1 2026 launches + residential unsold under construction, units. Source: NAPIC H1 2026, pp. 82 & 85.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMalaysia Residential Property Overhang: State of Play H1 2026 | NILAI Research (https://nilai.asia/research/malaysia-property-overhang)\nciteturn207search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 weeks ago; Crawled: today; The latest official picture of unsold completed residential homes in Malaysia, drawn from NAPIC's H1 2026 national report. ...   * Johor now has the highest residential overhang (4,222 units, 12.8% of the national total), just ahead of Selangor (4,185 units) and Perak (4,075 units).\n\nNILAI Research\n\n# Malaysia Residential Property Overhang: State of Play H1 2026\n\nThe latest official picture of unsold completed residential homes in Malaysia, drawn from NAPIC's H1 2026 national report.\n\nLatest data: H1 2026 (NAPIC national report); H2 2025 comparison Updated: 21 September 2026 Next update: when NAPIC publishes its next half-year report\n\nResidential overhang kept climbing in H1 2026, reaching 33,094 completed units worth RM17.78 billion, extending an upward trend that started in H2 2024.\n\n  * This is the fourth straight half-year increase: national overhang has risen every period since H1 2024 (22,642 units), through 23,149, 26,911 and 30,471, to 33,094 now.\n  * NAPIC attributes this to persistent caution in the market, among both developers (fewer aggressive launches) and buyers (weaker take-up), not to financing costs: the central bank has held its policy rate steady at 2.75% throughout the period.\n  * Johor now has the highest residential overhang (4,222 units, 12.8% of the national total), just ahead of Selangor (4,185 units) and Perak (4,075 units).\n--- | --- | ---\nH1 2024  | 22,642  | (baseline)\nH2 2024  | 23,149  | +2.2%\nH1 2025  | 26,911  | +16.3%\nH2 2025  | 30,471  | +13.2%\nH1 2026  | 33,094  | +8.6%\n\nOverhang by state, H1 2026\n\nState  | Unsold completed  | Unsold under construction  | Unsold not yet built\n--- | --- | --- | ---\nWP Kuala Lumpur  | 3,687  | 2,498  | 2,851\nSelangor  | 4,185  | 15,665  | 994\nJohor  | 4,222  | 5,879  | 2,788\nPulau Pinang  | 3,114  | 7,100  | 135\nPerak  | 4,075  | 6,967  | 1,841\n\n## What to Watch\n\n  * Selangor's construction pipeline is the one to watch: 15,665 units are still unsold while under construction, more than double the next-highest state (Pulau Pinang, 7,100). None of this is counted as overhang yet.\n  * Affordable stock (below RM300,000) is the single largest price band in overhang, which cuts against any narrative that oversupply is purely a luxury-segment problem.\n  * The pace of increase is actually slowing: half-year growth has decelerated from +16.3% to +8.6% over the last three periods, even though the total keeps rising.\n\n## Sources\n\nNAPIC, Bank Negara Malaysia\n\nSome breakdowns (6) weren't available in this edition due to limited source coverage.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStephanie Yap - your expert property professional in Ara Damansara, Selangor, Malaysia | IQI Spain (https://iqiglobal.com/es/agent/how-yee)\nciteturn207search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; Approach us now and explore your property investment with better and clearer options today. [custom_blog_form] Continue reading: Johor Property Market Forecast 2027: What the JS-SEZ and RTS Link Actually Change Top 10 Developments that Near Johor–Singapore CIQ/RTS Link First-Time Home Buyer Guide Malaysia 2026: From Savings to Keys References 1. ... SOUTHERN REGION PROPERTY MARKET REPORT First Half 2026. https://napic.jpph.gov.my/storage/app/media//3penerbitan/Shahrul/Bahagian%20Pasaran%20Harta%20Tanah/Southern%20Region%20Wilayah%20Selatan/Q2%202026/Southern%20Region%20H1%202026.pdf 2.\nNew homes start from about RM380,000 for a city-centre SOHO, RM491,000 for a terrace in Pasir Gudang and RM624,000 for a terrace in Iskandar Puteri. Prime units can go above RM1 million. 5. Can Singaporeans buy property in Johor? Yes, but there are rules. Most areas have a minimum price of RM1 million, except selected new units in zones like Medini. Foreigners need state approval and pay 8% stamp duty. They cannot buy Bumiputera lots, Malay reserve land or low-cost homes. Ready to get your property details and guidance? Approach us now and explore your property investment with better and clearer options today. [custom_blog_form] Continue reading: Johor Property Market Forecast 2027: What the JS-SEZ and RTS Link Actually Change Top 10 Developments that Near Johor–Singapore CIQ/RTS Link First-Time Home Buyer Guide Malaysia 2026: From Savings to Keys References 1. National Property Information Centre (NAPIC). (2026, September). SOUTHERN REGION PROPERTY MARKET REPORT First Half 2026. https://napic.jpph.gov.my/storage/app/media//3penerbitan/Shahrul/Bahagian%20Pasaran%20Harta%20Tanah/Southern%20Region%20Wilayah%20Selatan/Q2%202026/Southern%20Region%20H1%202026.pdf 2. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nH1 2026 Malaysia Property Market Update (https://estatemarketpulse.com/2026/09/21/h1-2026-malaysia-property-market-update/)\nciteturn207search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 weeks ago; Crawled: 5 days ago; NAPIC LPH H1 2026, Charts 1–2.[ ... H1 2025’s volume engine was Selangor, Johor and Perak.\n\nImage: H1 2026 Malaysia Property Update\n\n## H1 2026 Malaysia Property Market Update\n\nIn H1 2026, Malaysia’s property market recorded 187,320 transactions valued at RM105.12 billion, marking declines in both volume and value compared to H1 2025.\n\nSeptember 21, 2026\n\nReal Estate Market Review\n\nIn H1 2026, Malaysia’s property market recorded a decline in both transaction and value compared to H1 2025.\n\nMarket Pulse · NAPIC H1 2026\n\n187,320 Transactions, H1 2026\n\nRM105.12b Value, H1 2026\n\n−4.5%Volume vs H1 2025 (196,232)\n\n−2.4%Value vs H1 2025 (RM107.68b)\n\nNAPIC’s first-half 2026 property market report is out. Malaysia recorded 187,320 transactions worth RM105.12 billion. In the same half of 2025 they were 196,232 deals and RM107.68 billion. Volume is down 4.5%, and value is down 2.4%.[1][2]\n\nThat is the first H1 where both volume and value fell since the market bounced in 2022. Even so, H1 2024 was already 198,906 deals and RM105.65 billion, so the slide did not start this year. H1 2025 was a bit lighter on volume as well.[1][2]\n\nGDP was 5.4% in the first quarter and then 6.0% in the second.[4] Meanwhile, the overnight policy rate stayed at 2.75%.[4] Budget 2026 kept the RM500,000 first-home stamp duty exemption until 31 December 2027 and lifted the SJKP housing-credit guarantee to RM20 billion.[1] Although those are the supportive lines, the numbers were down nonetheless.\n\n### National volume (transactions)\n\nH1 2024–25 H1 2026\n\nH1 2024  |  | 198,906\nH1 2025  |  | 196,232\nH1 2026  |  | 187,320\n\nH1 2024–H1 2026. NAPIC LPH H1 2026, Charts 1–2.[1]\n\n### National value (RM billion)\n\nH1 2024–25 H1 2026\n\nH1 2024  |  | 105.65b\nH1 2025  |  | 107.68b\nH1 2026  |  | 105.12b\n\nValue peaked in H1 2025, then eased.[1]\n\n## Residential sector has slowed down\n\nTerrace houses still lead residential volume at 40.9%, then high-rise 19.1%, vacant plots 16.9%, semi-D 7.7%, low-cost 6.6%, detached 6.4%.[1] The type mix did not suddenly change. Instead, the price mix is the story.\n\n  * Below RM300,000: 57,323 deals, down 10.2%.\n  * RM300,000 to RM500,000: 28,839, down 1.5%.\n  * RM500,000 to RM1 million: 18,357, down 9.4%.\n  * Above RM1 million: 6,479, down 6.6%.[1]\n\nIn particular, the slowdown is in the affordable market. The sub-RM300,000 range is “dominant” at 51.6% of residential and fell hardest.[1]\n\nJohor, Selangor, WP Kuala Lumpur and Pulau Pinang still did about half of national residential volume.[1]\n\n### Price bands\n\nPrice band  | H1 2025  | H1 2026  | Change\n--- | --- | --- | ---\nBelow RM300,000  | 63,847  | 57,323  | −10.2%\nRM300,000 – RM500,000  | 29,269  | 28,839  | −1.5%\nRM500,000 – RM1 million  | 20,254  | 18,357  | −9.4%\nAbove RM1 million  | 6,937  | 6,479  | −6.6%\n## Under construction update\n\n### New launches\n\nNew launches in H1 2026 were 27,832 units, against a revised 37,250 in H1 2025, down 25.3%. Units sold from those launches were 4,618 against 8,233. Sales performance was 16.6%, down from 22.1%.[1]\n\nTerrace made up 46.8% of launches (13,028 units) and high-rise 41.4% (11,535). Selangor launched the most, 8,354 units, and sold only 6.8%. Johor launched 6,697 and sold 36.5%. Pulau Pinang launched 2,878 and sold 9.8%.[1]\n\n### Pipeline\n\nMeanwhile, construction pulled back harder than launches. Completions were 35,909, down 15.4%. Incoming supply was 284,679, down 24.3%. Starts were 27,765, down 36.3%. That is the sharpest construction cut in the report. Even so, new planned supply is the only pipeline item that rose, up 24.8% to 30,469. Existing stock is 6.55 million units, up 2.2%.[1]\n\nKL starts declined 90.1% to 826 units. Melaka starts were down 74.8%, Penang 68.0%. By contrast, Selangor residential starts jumped 150.9% to 9,580,[1] an exception amid the overall decline.\n\nPipeline  | H1 2025  | H1 2026  | Change\n--- | --- | --- | ---\nLaunches (revised)  | 37,250  | 27,832  | −25.3%\nLaunch units sold  | 8,233  | 4,618  | −43.9%\nSales performance  | 22.1%  | 16.6%  | −5.5 ppt\nStarts  | 43,556  | 27,765  | −36.3%\nCompletions  | 42,431  | 35,909  | −15.4%\nPlanned supply  | —  | 30,469  | +24.8%\n\nUse H1 2025r launches (Table XX), not the 23,380 printed in the original H1 2025 report.[1][2]\n\n### Construction and primary market\n\nDown (left)Up (right)\n\nLaunches  |  | −25.3%\nUnits sold  |  | −43.9%\nStarts  |  | −36.3%\nCompletions  |  | −15.4%\nPlanned supply  |  | +24.8%\n\n### Launch take-up, selected states\n\nSelangor  |  | 6.8%\nPulau Pinang  |  | 9.8%\nJohor  |  | 36.5%\n\nSelangor launched the most and sold the least of the three.[1]\n\nState  | Launched  | Sold\n--- | --- | ---\nSelangor  | 8,354  | 6.8%\nJohor  | 6,697  | 36.5%\nPulau Pinang  | 2,878  | 9.8%\n\nOverview pp. 9–10.[1]\n\n## Industrial: fewer deals, more value = rising price\n\nIndustrial is the quality-over-quantity sector. Volume was 3,932, down 5.2%, while value was RM14.78 billion, up 3.8%. Terrace factory and warehouse was 34.1% of volume (1,339). Vacant industrial plots were 27.7% (1,088).[1]\n\nSelangor did 32.9% of industrial volume (1,293), Johor 17.0% (669), Negeri Sembilan 7.9% (309).[1] Northern industrial was the bright pocket: volume +12.0%, value +24.1%. NAPIC ties the demand channel in the text to E&E, AI and data centres.[1]\n\n### Development land\n\nDevelopment land grew with a narrow geography. Volume rose 5.3% to 13,918 and value rose 3.3% to RM11.23 billion. Johor development-land value was +56.1%, Sarawak +71.0%, Sabah +133.8%, Kelantan volume +43.5%. In contrast, Selangor, Perak and Melaka development-land value fell hard.[1] “Land is strong” is a Johor and East Malaysia sentence, not a national one.\n\nIndustrial  | H1 2025  | H1 2026  | Change\n--- | --- | --- | ---\nVolume (deals)  | 4,148  | 3,932  | −5.2%\nValue (RM b)  | 14.25  | 14.78  | +3.8%\nUnsold completed  | 696  | 482  | −30.7%\nStarts  | —  | 1,038  | +26.1%\nCompletions  | —  | 243  | −31.2%\n\nStarts +26.1% to 1,038. Completions 243 (−31.2%).[1]\n\n### Industrial H1 2026 vs H1 2025\n\nDown (left)Up (right)\n\nVolume  |  | −5.2%\nValue  |  | +3.8%\nUnsold completed  |  | −30.7%\nStarts  |  | +26.1%\nCompletions  |  | −31.2%\n\n## The market centres: overall decline\n\nH1 2025’s volume engine was Selangor, Johor and Perak. However, in H1 2026 those three all fell. East Coast and East Malaysia were the only regions with both volume and value up. Even so, value is still a Central plus Southern story: 70.5% of national value share.[1]\n\n### Central\n\nCentral (KL, Selangor, Putrajaya): 42,665 deals, down 6.1%; RM43.41 billion, down 7.2%. That is 22.8% of national volume and 41.3% of national value. Selangor 31,714 deals, down 9.2%. WP KL 10,772, up 4.2% in volume but down 8.7% in value (fewer large commercial tickets than a year earlier). Putrajaya value was down 46.9%.[1]\n\n### Southern\n\nSouthern (Johor, Melaka, Negeri Sembilan): 45,956 deals, down 10.1%; RM30.73 billion, up 3.7%. The split is Johor: volume fell 11.5% to 28,238 while value rose 8.2% to RM23.43 billion. Residential volume in the state was down 15.1% (21,061 to 17,871), the single largest state-sector volume drop in the country. Offsetting that: Johor commercial value +25.8% and development-land value +56.1%. KL overtook Penang. Pahang overtook Negeri Sembilan and Melaka. Penang is the only major state to lose a top-8 slot. Value ranking is Selangor RM28.31 billion, Johor RM23.43 billion, WP KL RM14.90 billion. NAPIC flags volume-up states as WP KL, Terengganu, Pahang, Kelantan, Sabah, Sarawak and WP Labuan.[1]\n\nRegion  | H1 2025  | H1 2026  | Vol\n--- | --- | --- | ---\nCentral  | 45,442  | 42,665  | −6.1%\nSouthern  | 51,128  | 45,956  | −10.1%\nNorthern  | 50,286  | 46,381  | −7.8%\nEast Coast  | 32,433  | 33,652  | +3.8%\nEast Malaysia  | 16,943  | 18,666  | +10.2%\n\nVolume, sum of Table III–IV states.[1]\n\nRegion  | H1 2025 RM b  | H1 2026 RM b  | Val\n--- | --- | --- | ---\nCentral  | 46.79  | 43.41  | −7.2%\nSouthern  | 29.62  | 30.73  | +3.7%\nNorthern  | 18.00  | 16.36  | −9.1%\nEast Coast  | 6.29  | 6.74  | +7.1%\nEast Malaysia  | 6.97  | 7.89  | +13.1%\n\nValue, sum of Table III–IV states.[1]\n\n### Regions, volume and value change\n\nDown (left)Up (right)\n\nCentral vol  |  | −6.1%\nCentral val  |  | −7.2%\nSouthern vol  |  | −10.1%\nSouthern val  |  | +3.7%\nNorthern vol  |  | −7.8%\nNorthern val  |  | −9.1%\nEast Coast vol  |  | +3.8%\nEast Coast val  |  | +7.1%\nEast Malaysia vol  |  | +10.2%\nEast Malaysia val  |  | +13.1%\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNew Property Launch KL & Malaysia 2026: Projects & Prices (https://www.superhomes.my/resources/new-property-launches-malaysia-2026)\nciteturn207search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Crawled: today; (Source: NAPIC Property Market Status Report H1 2026; checked 1 Oct 2026.) ... Selangor launched 8,354 new homes in H1 2026, more than any other state.\n\n# All New Property Launches in Malaysia, State by State\n\nSH\n\nSuperHomes Editorial\n\nAI-assisted guide. How we write and check our guides\n\nPublished 2026-03-27 · Last reviewed 2026-10-01\n\nBaca panduan ini dalam Bahasa Malaysia\n\nImage: New Property Launch KL & Malaysia 2026: Projects & Prices\n\nDevelopers launched 27,832 new homes in Malaysia in H1 2026, 25.3% fewer than a year earlier, and only 16.6% of them sold in the period. Selangor led with 8,354 units, followed by Johor (6,697), Penang (2,878) and Kuala Lumpur (1,610). (Source: NAPIC Property Market Status Report H1 2026; checked 1 Oct 2026.) This guide lists notable named launches in each state, with developer, price and status, so you can compare options in one place.\n\nWhether you are looking for a new condo in KL, a landed home in Selangor or a mixed development in Penang, the information below will help you narrow down your choices. Every named project is taken from developer announcements or press reports that we checked on 1 October 2026, with sources listed at the end. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nKL Property Overhang 2026: What NAPIC's Data Shows | Kevin Teh (https://kevintehrealestate.com/kl-property-overhang-napic-q1-2026/)\nciteturn207search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 weeks ago; Crawled: 5 days ago; Johor is again the highest at 4,222 units, followed by Selangor at 4,185, Perak at 4,075, Kuala Lumpur at 3,687 and Penang at 3,114. ... Sources: NAPIC / JPPH Property Market Q1 2026 Snapshot; NAPIC Property Market Status Reports 2025 and H1 2026 — napic.jpph.gov.my/en/latest-publication; Global Property Guide — Malaysia residential market.\n\n# KL Property Overhang 2026: What NAPIC’s Data Shows\n\nImage: KL Property Overhang 2026: What NAPIC's Data Shows\n\nUpdate, 21 September 2026: NAPIC’s H1 2026 Property Market Status report, released on 10 September, shows 33,094 completed unsold residential units worth RM17.78 billion. Johor is again the highest at 4,222 units, followed by Selangor at 4,185, Perak at 4,075, Kuala Lumpur at 3,687 and Penang at 3,114. The Q1 2026 analysis below is unchanged and remains correct for that quarter.\n\nMalaysia’s unsold-property discussion has, for most of the last decade, been a discussion about Johor. NAPIC’s Q1 2026 figures indicate that framing no longer holds.\n\nNationally, completed unsold residential units stood at 32,801, worth RM16.37 billion — up from 30,471 at the end of 2025. A further 19,263 completed serviced apartments remained unsold, worth RM16.52 billion. Combined, more than 52,000 finished homes are held without a buyer, representing close to RM33 billion in value.\n\nThe state distribution is where the conventional account breaks down. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMalaysia House Prices Are Still Rising, But Only 16.6% of New Launches Sold — What the H1 2026 Data Actually Means (https://yourfinancedoc.com/en/malaysia-property-market-2026/)\nciteturn207search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 weeks ago; Crawled: today; The three states with the most new launches in H1 2026 were Selangor (8,354 units, 30% of the national total, 6.8% sales performance), Johor (6,697 units, 36.5% sales performance) and Pulau Pinang (2,878 units, 9.8% sales performance). ... This article's data is drawn primarily from the official report of the National Property Information Centre (NAPIC), Jabatan Penilaian dan Perkhidmatan Harta: LAPORAN PASARAN HARTA H1 2026 (napic.jpph.gov.my, released 10 September 2026), cross-checked against Bernama/RTM, Malay Mail and The Star's reporting of the same release.\n## 27,832 New Launches, But Only 16.6% Sales Performance\n\nNAPIC data shows 27,832 new residential units were launched in H1 2026, down sharply from the revised H1 2025 figure of 37,250 units. The sales performance for this launch cohort was 16.6%.\n\n16.6% does not mean \"83.4% will never sell\"\n\nThis figure represents the sales performance NAPIC recorded for the H1 2026 launch cohort specifically. It should not be read as \"5 out of every 6 new homes have no buyer,\" and it should not be treated as property overhang. Selling a home takes time. A project launched partway through H1 2026 that hasn't sold out by 30 June doesn't mean it never will. The more accurate reading is that current absorption of new launches is running slower than usual, not that the market has collapsed.\n\nNew launches skew toward terraced houses and high-rise, not evenly split\n\nOf the 27,832 new units launched in H1 2026:\n\n  * Terraced houses: 13,028 units, 46.8%, of which 4,218 were single-storey and 8,810 were two-to-three-storey, concentrated mainly in Johor and Selangor;\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCompare Asian markets — ThonInvest (https://thoninvest.com/research/compare)\nciteturn207search8 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; MIDA via The Star — Selangor RM70bn H1 2026 (digital lead) · Checked Aug 2026 · Update sensitivity: med\nOpen source →\n\nOpen →\n  * MY MIDA states H1 Selangor · Johor · Penang before land hunt.\n\nImageMY\n\nApproved — Selangor H1 70 RM bn\n\nMIDA via The Star — Selangor RM70bn H1 2026 (digital lead) · Checked Aug 2026 · Update sensitivity: med\n\nOpen source →\n\nImageMY\n\nApproved — Johor H1 59.4 RM bn\n\nMIDA — Johor RM59.4bn H1 2026 (JS-SEZ) · Checked Aug 2026 · Update sensitivity: med\n\nOpen source →\n\nImageMY\n\nApproved — Penang H1 20.2 RM bn\n\nMIDA — Penang RM20.2bn H1 2026 (semicon / adv mfg) · Checked Aug 2026 · Update sensitivity: med\n\nOpen source →\n\nImageMY\n\nApproved — KL H1 26.6 RM bn\n\nMIDA — W.P. KL RM26.6bn H1 2026 · Checked Aug 2026 · Update sensitivity: med\n\nOpen source →\n\nImageMY\n\nData centre & cloud H1 95.8 RM bn\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWill House Prices Drop in Malaysia? What the Data Shows (https://malaysiaproperty4u.com/buying-guide/property-prices-what-the-data-shows/)\nciteturn207search9 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 days ago; Crawled: today; The published data describes the past only: as at 30 June 2026 (NAPIC, released 10 September 2026) the house price index was up 0.9% year-on-year and down 0.5% on the quarter, averaging RM506,317; Johor rose 3.6% while Selangor fell 1.1%; transaction volume fell 4.5%; 33,094 completed homes were unsold; and the OPR has been 2.75% since 9 July 2025. ... NAPIC / JPPH — Malaysian House Price Index Q1–Q2 2026P (PDF, cover title; the publication index lists it as H1 2026)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNorth Klang Valley Property Trends: MHPI Data & Capital Appreciation in Gamuda Gardens | Gamuda Gardens (https://gamudagardens.com.my/articles/north-klang-valley-property-trends-mhpi-data-capital-appreciation-in-gamuda-gardens)\nciteturn207search10 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 weeks ago; Crawled